[Music]  this morning we're hearing the dramatic  story of an afghan interpreter who  served alongside the u.s military but is  now struggling to get a visa to escape  to the united states our cbs news  investigative unit spoke with the man  who is in hiding and also with a u.s  marine who saw combat alongside this  translator  senior investigative correspondent  katherine harris reports now on the man  and his family and how they fear they  could soon be killed  he's an incredibly honorable man who who  served our country at great peril to his  own life former marine mike donahue is  talking about the afghan translator who  embedded with his unit in 2009 who will  call sherzod for security reasons how  much time does sherzod and his family  have could be hours could be days  i'm just hoping he makes it back this is  one of his letters of appreciation  documents reviewed by cbs news suggest  sherzod applied for a special immigrant  visa as early as 2016.  donahue told us the process was botched  and he resubmitted sherzod's papers  along with multiple commendations from  the u.s military every moment i'm in  danger we reached sherzod where he's  hiding in a safe house in afghanistan  that's littered with garbage and filth  we agreed to blur his face every moment  i feel taliban will find out and you  know they will kill us  [Applause]  after nearly escaping last month's  suicide bombing at the kabul airport  sherzod fled the city with his wife and  children i'm scared about my family kids  that's it when shirzad told us the  taliban are hunting translators his  feelings overtook him please expedite  our our process  exploit our process you know  we are almost getting killed man  watching that video is an extremely  emotional thing i mean god what did we  do to these people what did we do we  left them we abandoned them a state  department spokesperson would not  address sherzod's case but told cbs news  they are processing special immigrant  visas at an accelerated 